1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:04,749
Can one language model learn several specialist skills

2
00:00:04,819 --> 00:00:07,052
without retraining all of its weights?

3
00:00:07,182 --> 00:00:12,279
This is Adaptive LoRA Lab, my local machine learning research project.

4
00:00:13,709 --> 00:00:16,821
A pretrained model is a useful starting point,

5
00:00:16,891 --> 00:00:21,343
but reliable tool calls and working code need more than fluent text.

6
00:00:21,473 --> 00:00:24,076
The challenge was to improve specific tasks

7
00:00:24,146 --> 00:00:27,172
within the limits of a single laptop GPU.

8
00:00:28,602 --> 00:00:32,208
My goal was to build small, interchangeable adapters

9
00:00:32,278 --> 00:00:36,475
around one frozen Qwen3 eight-billion-parameter base model.

10
00:00:36,605 --> 00:00:40,354
Success meant measurable gains, controlled comparisons,

11
00:00:40,424 --> 00:00:43,916
and a working demo with evidence behind every result.

12
00:00:45,346 --> 00:00:50,189
I started with reproducible data preparation: normalize tool schemas,

13
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:54,817
preserve official test sets, and freeze training and validation splits.

14
00:00:54,947 --> 00:00:57,757
Then I trained four-bit QLoRA adapters,

15
00:00:57,827 --> 00:01:01,586
saving configurations, checkpoints, and raw predictions.

16
00:01:03,016 --> 00:01:05,873
Early experiments exposed a useful failure:

17
00:01:05,943 --> 00:01:08,732
low training loss, but invalid tool calls.

18
00:01:08,862 --> 00:01:12,017
I investigated output tokens and supervision,

19
00:01:12,087 --> 00:01:15,131
then repaired completion training through the end token

20
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:19,216
and jointly updated adapter layers, including the output head.

21
00:01:20,646 --> 00:01:23,344
Next came controlled studies of adapter rank,

22
00:01:23,414 --> 00:01:26,446
training data size, and coding target layers.

23
00:01:26,576 --> 00:01:31,397
Validation selected rank eight and three thousand tool training examples

24
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:35,987
before the final test. Repeat seeds checked how much results varied.

25
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:41,190
I connected tool and coding adapters to one shared base,

26
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,756
with manual switching and a lightweight rule-based router.

27
00:01:44,886 --> 00:01:49,247
A bilingual interface exposes the full fourteen-stage pipeline,

28
00:01:49,317 --> 00:01:53,180
saved errors, and isolated mock-tool or Docker checks.

29
00:01:54,610 --> 00:02:00,065
The selected tool adapter achieved 92.92 percent exact calls

30
00:02:00,135 --> 00:02:03,192
across 961 official cases.

31
00:02:03,322 --> 00:02:06,922
All cases passed format and mock execution checks.

32
00:02:07,052 --> 00:02:09,612
Sixty-eight exact-match errors remained:

33
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:12,462
executable does not always mean correct.

34
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:18,047
The first coding pilot did not beat the base on HumanEval,

35
00:02:18,117 --> 00:02:20,167
and I kept that negative result.

36
00:02:20,297 --> 00:02:25,779
The expanded adapter later passed 111 of 164 tasks,

37
00:02:25,849 --> 00:02:29,926
compared with 98 for the base: thirteen additional passes.

38
00:02:31,356 --> 00:02:35,460
On 465 eligible MBPP tasks,

39
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:39,364
three coding seeds averaged 53.62 percent.

40
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:43,343
Independent CPU replay verified saved outputs.

41
00:02:43,473 --> 00:02:47,548
These results describe specific benchmarks and training variation;

42
00:02:47,678 --> 00:02:50,764
they do not establish universal superiority.

43
00:02:52,194 --> 00:02:55,187
The router matched all twenty-seven route contracts.

44
00:02:55,317 --> 00:03:01,001
In a small integration pilot, automated routing passed 22 of 24 checks,

45
00:03:01,071 --> 00:03:03,863
versus 14 using only the base.

46
00:03:03,993 --> 00:03:07,120
Each total includes five clarification cases.

47
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:12,432
The result is a completed local research and demo project:

48
00:03:12,502 --> 00:03:15,951
reproducible experiments, specialist adaptation,

49
00:03:16,021 --> 00:03:18,933
transparent routing, and auditable results.

50
00:03:19,063 --> 00:03:22,567
For me, the key achievement is connecting model development

51
00:03:22,637 --> 00:03:26,015
with rigorous evaluation and a usable product.
